# Decisions in verdict functions that no self-test pins — LESSONS #069, #071.
#
# harnesses/gate-mutation/mutate_gates.py forces every decision in a verdict
# function (one holding a mutation row's target) True, then False, and runs the
# harness's self-test. Each line below is a mutant that left it GREEN: either
# the gate behaves differently on some input and nothing notices, or the
# decision can never matter (an equivalent mutant); a line's `why` says which.
# The sweep fails on an unpinned decision missing from this file, and on a line
# here that no longer names one. So the file only shrinks: pin a decision with
# a fixture, then delete its line. Keys name a decision by function, source
# line and expression, never by line number; `n` numbers repeats on one line.
#
# Every line was triaged on 2026-09-26 (LESSONS #071). Of the 204 in the first
# baseline, 137 were pinned by fixtures (one by #070's, one by the resolver's
# conflict-marker fixture, the rest in #071) and 6 went with code that was fixed
# or removed. The 61 left, and one decision added since, say which kind each
# is, and why:
#   equivalent:  forcing it cannot change what the harness does.
#   report:      it changes only what a person reads; the verdict, the exit code
#                and every field another tool reads are the same.
#   stricter:    forced one way it only refuses more, and the permission it
#                guards is a design choice rather than documented behaviour.
# A decision that changes a verdict, an exit code or a machine-read field is
# never any of these: pin it. A new line needs its own reason of one kind.
